<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Dance · 1916 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Pelo Telefone</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Made up in 1916 by musicians meeting in Tia Ciata's backyard, it is counted as the first samba ever recorded in Brazil.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mauro de Almeida (attributed; a collective creation)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Donga (Ernesto dos Santos) (attributed; a collective creation)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Samba musicians of Rio de Janeiro (the circle at Tia Ciata's house)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Praça Onze (now Cidade Nova), Rio de Janeiro, Brazil</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1916</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (registered 27 November 1916 and recorded by Casa Edison (published before 1931); words attributed to Mauro de Almeida, music to Donga)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelo-telefone">Pelo Telefone</a>&quot; (&quot;On the Telephone&quot;) is held, according to the records of the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, to be the first samba song recorded in Brazil, although earlier recordings exist, such as &quot;Samba – Em Casa da Bahiana&quot; (1913) and &quot;Urubu Malandro&quot; (1914). It was made in 1916 in the backyard of Tia Ciata, in Praça Onze (now Cidade Nova), whose house, a famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> house, was frequented by the popular musicians of the time. First titled &quot;Roceiro&quot;, it was a collective creation, born of improvisation in a samba circle, with João da Baiana, Pixinguinha, Caninha, Hilário Jovino Ferreira, Sinhô and others taking part.</p><p>Its authorship has been disputed ever since. Donga (Ernesto dos Santos) registered it on 27 November 1916 under his own name alone, and later added the journalist Mauro de Almeida as his partner. He justified registering it with a saying attributed to Sinhô, that music is like a bird and belongs to whoever catches it first. Because the song was a huge success, various musicians claimed it. On 4 February 1917 the newspaper Jornal do Brasil printed a note from the Grêmio Fala Gente announcing that &quot;the true tango 'Pelo Telephone'&quot;, by João da Mata, Germano, Tia Ciata and Hilário, would be sung on Avenida Rio Branco, &quot;dedicated to the good and remembered friend Mauro&quot;.</p><p>The original words were later changed into the version best known today, in which the chief of police sends word by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> that there is a roulette wheel to play at the Carioca. Donga told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s Museum of Image and Sound that this was a parody made by the journalists of the newspaper A Noite: in 1913 its reporters had set up a roulette wheel in the Largo da Carioca to show how the police tolerated gambling. The musician and broadcaster Henrique Foréis Domingues confirmed this in the newspaper O Dia on 13 February 1972, and accused Donga of having taken a collective work for himself. Donga conceded that he had not written the words, which were Mauro de Almeida's, and blamed the record label: &quot;The omission of Mauro's name on the recording by Casa Edison cannot be attributed to me&quot;, he said.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light carnival samba. In its first words the chief of the revelry sends word by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> that the merrymaking is to be enjoyed without question; in the version best known today he becomes the chief of police, sending word that a roulette wheel is waiting in the Carioca, a mocking jab at the police's tolerance of gambling. The mood is playful and satirical.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Portuguese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Portuguese</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">O chefe da folia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The chief of the revelry</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Pelo telefone</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by telephone</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Mandou me avisar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sent word to tell me</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Que com alegria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that, with joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Não se questione</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let there be no arguing [?]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pt">Para se brincar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>about having fun.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Donga and Mauro de Almeida (1916), the original opening as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelo_Telefone" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'folia' is carnival merrymaking, revelry; 'o chefe da folia' is the leader of the revels.</li><li>lines 1.5-1.6: 'Não se questione / Para se brincar' is loosely put; read as 'let no one question (or quarrel over) joining in the fun'. 'questionar' can be 'to question' or 'to dispute, argue'; 'brincar' is 'to play', in carnival 'to revel'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
